Patric Mueller wrote:
> I learnt yesterday that Medusa petrifies the player even when he's
> hallucinating.
> 
> That was surprising to me. :)
> 
> The mythology is quite clear. It isn't Medusa's gaze that's
> petrifying but her look.
> 
> I know in NetHack it is programmed as an active monster attack but I
> always thought that the fact that blinding yourself protects you and
> the message "you meet Medusa's gaze" acknowledges the fact that her
> ugly look is the cause of stoning.

Not just in NH, but most modern gaming treatments of Medusa-type
monsters have converted the petrification effect to an active gaze attack.

> I don't know how much the DevTeam wants to follow the mythology.
> 
> After all Perseus wasn't stoned so how can there be his statue.

For the times when there's no shield in his statue, it's pretty obvious.
For the times when he's got one, it must have been in his pack rather
than equipped. Or something like that. Maybe he unequipped it to lower
the fail rate on his spells?

> Perseus used his shield to view Medusa as her reflected gaze has
> apparently lost its stoning property so how can she be stoned by her
> own gaze with a mirror?

This would lead into a way-way-OT discussion about whether looking upon
the image of something is the same as looking upon the thing itself.
Sticking with ancient Greece for the moment, elements of Plato's Cave
certainly come to mind.

> But being stoned while hallucinating feels to me like something the
> DevTeam doesn't have thought about.

There's just something about this statement that makes me want to run
out and get some brownies and a big bag of Cheetos. ;)


